WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.376
Everything you build with AI sits

2
00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:05.954
somewhere on one road: your computer,

3
00:00:05.954 --> 00:00:09.704
the network, the internet, services, and AI.

4
00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:11.701
When something breaks, the first useful

5
00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:14.069
question is which stop it's on.

6
00:00:14.069 --> 00:00:15.625
Learn that picture once,

7
00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:18.539
and every later lesson has a place to land.

8
00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.640
Think of it as a road leading out of your room.

9
00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:24.866
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

10
00:00:24.866 --> 00:00:27.559
in Orientation: what this course is,

11
00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:29.417
and the lantern you carry.

12
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:30.857
This is the view from the gate,

13
00:00:30.857 --> 00:00:33.318
before you walk into the six districts.

14
00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:35.199
One road, five stops,

15
00:00:35.199 --> 00:00:38.091
and lanterns strung over the whole thing.

16
00:00:38.541 --> 00:00:41.502
Stop one: your computer.

17
00:00:41.502 --> 00:00:44.659
Files, folders, programs, the terminal.

18
00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:46.378
Everything here is yours,

19
00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:49.199
and it still works with the Wi-Fi off.

20
00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:52.612
A lot of early confusion lives right here.

21
00:00:52.612 --> 00:00:54.330
Aaron, who made this course,

22
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.289
once saved his Python as a .txt file.

23
00:00:58.289 --> 00:00:59.868
The code was fine.

24
00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:02.215
The problem was at stop one.

25
00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:05.023
Stop two: the network.

26
00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:08.169
Your Wi-Fi, and the router: the box

27
00:01:08.169 --> 00:01:11.141
every device at home goes through on its way out.

28
00:01:11.141 --> 00:01:13.754
Stop three: the internet.

29
00:01:13.754 --> 00:01:15.763
Networks joined to other networks,

30
00:01:15.763 --> 00:01:17.063
all over the world.

31
00:01:17.063 --> 00:01:18.967
Nobody owns all of it.

32
00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:21.428
Stop four: services.

33
00:01:21.428 --> 00:01:24.574
GitHub, your email, a payment page.

34
00:01:24.574 --> 00:01:26.699
Each one is someone else's computer,

35
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:29.834
running all day, with its door open for requests.

36
00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:32.249
And stop five: AI.

37
00:01:32.249 --> 00:01:35.186
A model, usually running inside a service,

38
00:01:35.186 --> 00:01:36.987
answering in words.

39
00:01:37.437 --> 00:01:39.109
Follow one message.

40
00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:41.988
You type a question into an AI chat.

41
00:01:41.988 --> 00:01:44.682
Your computer hands it to the Wi-Fi,

42
00:01:44.682 --> 00:01:47.689
and the router passes it out to the internet.

43
00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.614
The internet carries it to the right service.

44
00:01:50.614 --> 00:01:53.204
The service checks it's really you,

45
00:01:53.204 --> 00:01:54.133
runs the model,

46
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:56.687
and sends the answer back down the road.

47
00:01:56.687 --> 00:01:58.486
Programs knock at a side counter,

48
00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:01.842
called an API, and show a key there.

49
00:02:01.842 --> 00:02:03.618
Here's where the picture breaks.

50
00:02:03.618 --> 00:02:05.963
It isn't really a straight line.

51
00:02:05.963 --> 00:02:08.296
Services call other services.

52
00:02:08.296 --> 00:02:11.338
And some AI models run right on your own computer,

53
00:02:11.338 --> 00:02:13.267
with no road at all.

54
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:15.958
Now the districts land on the road.

55
00:02:15.958 --> 00:02:18.918
The Machine and The Code live on your computer:

56
00:02:18.918 --> 00:02:22.541
files, the terminal, languages, packages.

57
00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:24.619
The History starts there too.

58
00:02:24.619 --> 00:02:27.463
Git saves your work on your own machine;

59
00:02:27.463 --> 00:02:30.075
GitHub is the service where it's shared.

60
00:02:30.075 --> 00:02:31.503
The Network is the road,

61
00:02:31.503 --> 00:02:33.581
and how things talk across it:

62
00:02:33.581 --> 00:02:38.388
networking, APIs, websites, the cloud.

63
00:02:38.388 --> 00:02:40.663
Trust guards the doors of services:

64
00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:43.450
keys, logins, secrets.

65
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.772
And Shipping is the day you stop only

66
00:02:45.772 --> 00:02:48.943
visiting services, and become one.

67
00:02:49.394 --> 00:02:51.321
Three phrases you'll meet early,

68
00:02:51.321 --> 00:02:53.260
and each one names a stop.

69
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.687
localhost means this computer.

70
00:02:55.687 --> 00:02:58.995
A tool running on localhost is in your own room,

71
00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:01.352
and nobody else can reach it yet.

72
00:03:01.352 --> 00:03:04.824
It works on my machine means two computers differ:

73
00:03:04.824 --> 00:03:07.076
different files, different versions,

74
00:03:07.076 --> 00:03:08.423
different settings.

75
00:03:08.423 --> 00:03:11.499
That gap is a lesson, not a flaw in you.

76
00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:13.868
And 401, Unauthorized,

77
00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:16.596
means you reached the service, but the door

78
00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:19.812
didn't accept your key, or you brought none.

79
00:03:19.812 --> 00:03:21.414
Your code may be fine.

80
00:03:21.414 --> 00:03:22.599
That's Trust.

81
00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:26.523
So when something breaks, ask one question first:

82
00:03:26.523 --> 00:03:28.986
which stop am I at?

83
00:03:29.435 --> 00:03:31.722
AI shows up at every stop.

84
00:03:31.722 --> 00:03:34.207
A coding agent edits files on your computer.

85
00:03:34.207 --> 00:03:36.714
It reaches its model across the internet.

86
00:03:36.714 --> 00:03:40.209
With MCP, it can reach other services for you.

87
00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:42.914
So it matters which stop it's standing at,

88
00:03:42.914 --> 00:03:44.621
and which keys it holds.

89
00:03:44.621 --> 00:03:47.488
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

90
00:03:47.488 --> 00:03:50.311
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

91
00:03:50.762 --> 00:03:53.212
Some things look important and can wait.

92
00:03:53.212 --> 00:03:55.418
How routers pick a path.

93
00:03:55.418 --> 00:03:57.542
What's inside a packet.

94
00:03:57.542 --> 00:03:59.887
Which cloud company is best.

95
00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:03.266
Every acronym on your network settings screen.

96
00:04:03.266 --> 00:04:05.529
For now, you only need to know

97
00:04:05.529 --> 00:04:07.644
which stop you're standing at.

98
00:04:08.094 --> 00:04:10.044
Three things to keep.

99
00:04:10.044 --> 00:04:12.517
Everything sits on one road:

100
00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.490
your computer, the network, the internet,

101
00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.589
services, and AI lighting all of it.

102
00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.679
Ask which stop first.

103
00:04:20.679 --> 00:04:22.722
A problem in your room and a problem

104
00:04:22.722 --> 00:04:25.486
at a service's door need different fixes.

105
00:04:25.486 --> 00:04:27.657
A service is someone else's computer,

106
00:04:27.657 --> 00:04:29.294
with its door open.

107
00:04:29.294 --> 00:04:31.674
Most of the AI you use lives there,

108
00:04:31.674 --> 00:04:33.707
and answers back to yours.
